&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;DOCUMENTATION REQUIREMENTS&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Section 1833(e) of the Social Security Act precludes payment to any provider of services unless &quot;there has been furnished such information as may be necessary in order to determine the amounts due such provider.&amp;#8221; It is expected that the beneficiary&#39;s medical records will reflect the need for the care provided. The beneficiary&#39;s medical records include the treating practitioner&#39;s office records, hospital records, nursing home records, home health agency records, records from other healthcare professionals and test reports. This documentation must be available upon request.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GENERAL DOCUMENTATION REQUIREMENTS&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;In order to justify payment for DMEPOS items, suppliers must meet the following requirements:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;SWO&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Medical Record Information (including continued need&sol;use if applicable)&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Correct Coding&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Proof of Delivery&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;Refer to the LCD-related Standard Documentation Requirements article, located at the bottom of this policy under the Related Local Coverage Documents section for additional information regarding these requirements.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Refer to the Supplier Manual for additional information on documentation requirements.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Refer to the DME MAC web sites for additional bulletin articles and other publications related to this LCD.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;POLICY SPECIFIC DOCUMENTATION REQUIREMENTS&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Items covered in this LCD have additional policy-specific requirements that must be met prior to Medicare reimbursement. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Refer to the LCD-related Policy Article, located at the bottom of this policy under the Related Local Coverage Documents section for additional information.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As a condition of payment pursuant to 42 CFR 410.38, Power Mobility Devices (PMDs) require a standard written order prior to delivery (WOPD)&amp;#160;for the base item. If the supplier does not receive the order&sol;prescription for the base item prior to delivery, the claim will be denied as not reasonable and necessary.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The WOPD for the base item may only be written after the completion of the face-to-face encounter requirements. Pursuant to the Social Security Act, Title XVIII, &amp;#167;1834(a)(1)(E)(iv), the treating practitioner who completes the face-to-face requirements must be the same practitioner who writes the order&sol;prescription for the PMD (base item).&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A supplier may provide a template to the treating practitioner for their use in creating the WOPD for the base item. Such a template may list the elements of a WOPD, but the supplier must not fill in or complete any of these elements.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An SWO is required prior to claim submission for all options, accessories, and&sol;or supplies that are separately billed in addition to the base. This SWO obtained prior to claim submission, may be prepared by someone other than a treating practitioner. If someone other than a treating practitioner prepares the SWO for separately billed options, accessories, and&sol;or supplies, a treating practitioner must review and sign the order.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The treating practitioner who reviews and signs the SWO for separately billable options, accessories, and&sol;or supplies does not need to be the same treating practitioner who completed the WOPD for the PMD base and conducted the face-to-face encounter. In this situation, the treating practitioner who orders the options, accessories, and&sol;or supplies must:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Verify that a qualifying face-to-face encounter occurred within 6-months prior to the date of the WOPD for the base item; and,&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Have documentation of the qualifying face-to-face encounter that was conducted for the base item;&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Review and sign their order.&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;Refer to the LCD-related Policy Article NON-MEDICAL NECESSITY COVERAGE AND PAYMENT RULES section for additional information.&lt;br&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;br&gt;FACE-TO-FACE ENCOUNTER:&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The face-to-face encounter must be conducted within six (6) months prior to the order date on the WOPD for the PMD (base item).&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;line-height: 115%;&quot;&gt;The report of the face-to-face encounter (refer to the LCD-related Policy Article) should provide information relating to the following questions.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;&sol;span&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;table style=&quot;width: 1191px; height: 129px;&quot; border=&quot;solid&quot;&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 197.016px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;For POVs, For PWCs,&lt;&sol;strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;or For POVs and PWCs&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 965.422px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Question&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;&sol;tr&gt;
&lt;&sol;thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 197.016px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;For POVs and PWCs&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 965.422px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;What is this beneficiary&amp;#8217;s mobility limitation and how does it interfere with the performance of activities of daily living?&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;&sol;tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 197.016px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;For POVs and PWCs&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 965.422px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;Why can&amp;#8217;t a cane or walker meet this beneficiary&amp;#8217;s mobility needs in the home?&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;&sol;tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 197.016px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;For POVs and PWCs&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 965.422px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;Why can&amp;#8217;t a manual wheelchair meet this beneficiary&amp;#8217;s mobility needs in the home?&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;&sol;tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 197.016px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;For POVs&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 965.422px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;Does this beneficiary have the physical and mental abilities to transfer into a POV and to operate it safely in the home?&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;&sol;tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 197.016px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;For PWCs&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 965.422px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;Why can&amp;#8217;t a POV (scooter) meet this beneficiary&amp;#8217;s mobility needs in the home?&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;&sol;tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 197.016px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;For PWCs&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;td style=&quot;width: 965.422px; border: 1px solid black;&quot;&gt;Does this beneficiary have the physical and mental abilities to operate a power wheelchair safely in the home?&lt;&sol;td&gt;
&lt;&sol;tr&gt;
&lt;&sol;tbody&gt;
&lt;&sol;table&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;The report should provide pertinent information about the following elements, but may include other details. Each element would not have to be addressed in every evaluation.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;History of the present condition(s) and past medical history that is relevant to mobility needs&lt;br&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Symptoms that limit ambulation&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Diagnoses that are responsible for these symptoms&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Medications or other treatment for these symptoms&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Progression of ambulation difficulty over time&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Other diagnoses that may relate to ambulatory problems&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How far the beneficiary can walk without stopping&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pace of ambulation&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What ambulatory assistance (cane, walker, wheelchair, caregiver) is currently used&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What has changed to now require use of a power mobility device&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ability to stand up from a seated position without assistance&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Description of the home setting and the ability to perform activities of daily living in the home&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Physical examination that is relevant to mobility needs&lt;br&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Weight and height&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Cardiopulmonary examination&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Musculoskeletal examination&lt;br&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Arm and leg strength and range of motion&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Neurological examination&lt;br&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Gait&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Balance and coordination&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;&sol;li&gt;
&lt;&sol;ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The evaluation should be tailored to the individual beneficiary&amp;#8217;s conditions. The history should paint a picture of the beneficiary&amp;#8217;s functional abilities and limitations on a typical day. It should contain as much objective data as possible. The physical examination should be focused on the body systems that are responsible for the beneficiary&amp;#8217;s ambulatory difficulty or impact on the beneficiary&#39;s ambulatory ability.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The written report of this encounter must be available upon request.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Practitioners shall document the encounter in a detailed narrative note in their charts in the format that they use for other entries. The note must clearly indicate that a major reason for the visit was a mobility encounter. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Many suppliers have created forms which have not been approved by CMS which they send to practitioners and ask them to complete. Even if the treating practitioner completes this type of form and puts it in his&sol;her chart, this supplier-generated form is &lt;u&gt; not&lt;&sol;u&gt; a substitute for the comprehensive medical record as noted above. Suppliers are encouraged to help educate practitioners on the type of information that is needed to document a beneficiary&#39;s mobility needs.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Practitioners shall also provide reports of pertinent laboratory tests, x-rays, and&sol;or other diagnostic tests (e.g., pulmonary function tests, cardiac stress test, electromyogram, etc.) performed in the course of management of the beneficiary. Upon request, suppliers shall provide notes from prior visits to give a historical perspective of the progression of disease over time and to corroborate the information in the face-to-face encounter.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;If the report of an LCMP examination is to be considered as part of the face-to-face encounter (refer to the LCD-related Policy Article), there must be a signed and dated attestation by the supplier or LCMP that the LCMP has no financial relationship with the supplier. (Note: Evaluations performed by an LCMP who has a financial relationship with the supplier may be submitted to provide additional clinical information, but will not be considered as part of the face-to-face encounter by the treating practitioner.)&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Although beneficiaries who qualify for coverage of a power mobility device may use that device outside the home, because Medicare&amp;#8217;s coverage of a wheelchair or POV is determined solely by the beneficiary&amp;#8217;s mobility needs within the home, the encounter must clearly distinguish the beneficiary&amp;#8217;s abilities and needs within the home from any additional needs for use outside the home.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;SPECIALTY EVALUATION:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The specialty evaluation that is required for beneficiaries who receive non-complex Group 2 seat elevator PWCs, Group 2 Single Power Option or Multiple Power Options PWC, any Group 3 PWC, any Group 5 PWC, or a power assist system is in addition to the requirement for the face-to-face encounter. The specialty evaluation provides detailed information explaining why each specific option or accessory &amp;#8211; i.e., power seat elevation system, power seating system, alternate drive control interface, or power assist system &amp;#8211; is needed to address the beneficiary&amp;#8217;s mobility limitation. There must be a written report of this evaluation available on request.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;HOME ASSESSMENT:&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Prior to or at the time of delivery of a POV or PWC, the supplier or practitioner must perform an on-site evaluation of the beneficiary&amp;#8217;s home to verify that the beneficiary can adequately maneuver the device that is provided considering physical layout, doorway width, doorway thresholds, and surfaces. There must be a written report of this evaluation available on request.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Miscellaneous&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Utilization Guidelines&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Refer to Coverage Indications, Limitations, and&sol;or Medical Necessity&lt;&sol;p&gt;

