&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;&sol;strong&gt;&lt;&sol;em&gt;&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The certainty of evidence for the use of power seat elevation systems in beneficiaries utilizing non-complex power wheelchairs to promote successful transfers, reduce shoulder injury associated with improper transferring biomechanics, reduce fall event incidence, and improve MRADLs is very low. The available studies are limited by observational design, imprecision, and indirectness, including populations primarily comprised of users of complex rehabilitative power wheelchairs rather than non-complex devices. No evidence was identified that assessed the effect of power seat elevator systems for non-complex PWC users on patient satisfaction or quality of life measures.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There is substantial heterogeneity among beneficiaries using non-complex power wheelchairs, including variation in underlying diagnoses, functional capacity, and the amount of time spent in the wheelchair, which complicates generalization of study findings across this population.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Despite these limitations, indirect evidence supports a plausible clinical benefit of seat elevation systems. Studies conducted in populations of predominantly Group 3 power wheelchair users demonstrate improvements in functional reach, transfer performance, and reduction in fall events, and mechanistic studies indicate reduced upper extremity strain during reaching and transfer-related activities. Given the overlap in clinical characteristics and functional limitations between some non-complex PWC and complex rehabilitative PWC users, these findings and the associated biomechanical mechanisms may be at least partially applicable to selected individuals using non-complex PWCs, including those with bilateral lower limb amputations.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Although derived from populations using more complex devices, the underlying biomechanical mechanisms by which seat elevation improves transfer height alignment, reduces required upper extremity force, and enhances reach capability are not specific to wheelchair classification. Therefore, it is reasonable to consider that these functional benefits may be transferable to appropriately selected beneficiaries using non-complex PWCs when similar time spent in wheelchair and physical limitations are present. Non-complex power wheelchairs with seat elevation systems may result in a net health benefit for Medicare beneficiaries with substantial mobility limitations whose primary means of mobility within the home is the power wheelchair and who rely upon their upper extremities for transfers and MRADLs.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Given the heterogeneity of clinical presentations and the absence of high-certainty direct evidence, determination of medical necessity for a seat elevation system should rely on clinical judgment. In this context, evaluation by a licensed&sol;certified medical professional and a qualified rehabilitation professional who specializes in wheelchairs, including RESNA-certified Assistive Technology Professionals, is critical to ensure that the beneficiary&amp;#8217;s needs are appropriately assessed and that access to medically necessary equipment is supported.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Review of the literature on the sequelae of bilateral lower limb amputations that predispose individuals to pressure injuries and restrict independent non-level transfers aligns with the evidence basis considered in the National Coverage Analysis (NCA) (CAG-00461N). The available evidence supports the conclusion that tilt, recline, and seat elevation functions are reasonable and necessary accessories on complex rehabilitation power wheelchairs to address biomechanical and safety challenges in individuals at increased risk of pressure injuries due to an impaired ability to independently perform weight shifts and transfers, including beneficiaries with bilateral lower limb amputations.&lt;&sol;p&gt;
